Solution:

1. Install the JWT Editor extension from the Burp BApp Store.
2. Log in using:
   wiener:peter
3. In Burp → Proxy → HTTP history, find the:
   GET /my-account
   request.
4. Send the request to Burp Repeater.
5. Copy the JWT session cookie and save it as the first token.
6. Log out and log in again using:
   wiener:peter
7. Copy the new JWT session cookie and save it as the second token.
8. Run:
   docker run --rm -it portswigger/sig2n <token1> <token2>
9. From the output, copy the tampered JWT from the first X.509 entry.
10. In Burp Repeater, change the request path to:
    GET /my-account
11. Replace the session cookie with the tampered JWT and send the request.
12. If you receive:
    200 OK
    the corresponding X.509 key is correct.
13. If you receive:
    302 → /login
    try the tampered JWT corresponding to the next X.509 key from the sig2n output.
14. Repeat until you identify the correct X.509 key.
15. Copy the Base64-encoded X.509 public key corresponding to the correct candidate.
16. Go to JWT Editor Keys and click New Symmetric Key.
17. Click Generate.
18. Replace the value of the k property with the Base64-encoded X.509 public key.
19. Save the key.
20. Return to the request in Burp Repeater and change the path to:
    GET /admin
21. Open the JSON Web Token tab.
22. Make sure the alg parameter is:
    "alg": "HS256"

23. Change the sub claim to:
    "sub": "administrator"
24. Click Sign and select the symmetric key generated earlier.
25. Make sure Don't modify header is selected, then click OK.
26. Send the request:
    GET /admin
27. Find the delete endpoint:
    /admin/delete?username=carlos
28. Send:
    GET /admin/delete?username=carlos
29. The lab is solved.
